  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Retirement, the withdrawal from active working life, generally targets age 65–67, though it can begin as early as 62 for reduced social security benefits. For those born in 1960 or later, full retirement age is 67. A secure retirement usually requires 70–90% of pre-retirement income, often achieved by saving 10–12 times annual income.Full Retirement Age for Social Security (FRA)Born 1954 or earlier: 65–66.Born 1955–1959: 66 and 2–10 months.Born 1960 or later: 67.Early Retirement: Benefits can start at 62 but are permanently reduced.Delayed Retirement: Increasing benefits by ~8% annually up to age 70.Retirement Planning and NeedsSavings Target: Aim for 10–12 times your final annual income.Income Replacement: Retirees generally need 70–90% of their annual preretirement income to maintain their standard of living.Gap Analysis: Calculate your gap by subtracting guaranteed income (Social Security/pensions) from your desired annual spending.Actionable Steps: Use the Social Security Administration's Retirement Estimator to calculate benefits and start saving early.Retirement BenefitsSocial Security: Replaces approximately 40% of the average wage earner's income.Medicare: Eligibility begins at age 65.Pension/401k/Roth: Additional investment vehicles essential for supplementing Social Security.Key ConsiderationsAverage Age: While many retire in their mid-60s, \"retirement\" can take many forms, including semi-retirement.Working While Retired: You can work and receive benefits, but your benefits may be temporarily reduced if you are under full retirement age and earn over a certain limit.",
